Fix the endpoint use-after-free when a web process dies.

ipc_endpoint_connect_to_socket() took no reference for the connection, but
ipc_hup() dropped one -- so the HUP that fires when a web process exits or
crashes stole the OWNER's reference.  One dead web process freed the
endpoint while the webview still held d->ipc; every later send then wrote
through the dangling pointer into whatever had reused the allocation.
Observed as a deterministic SIGSEGV in g_queue_push_tail (the freed slice
reused by a lua-serialize buffer, so the "queue" was message payload bytes),
as "corrupted double-linked list" heap damage in the UI process, and as
storms of "Trying to send an ipc message, but the endpoint went away."
-- whose handling ALSO leaked the message's reference.

The connection now holds its own reference; ipc_hup() disconnects (removing
both watches, so it cannot fire twice) and drops exactly that reference.
The send thread drops the message's reference on both outcomes.  ipc_send()
tolerates a NULL queue (endpoint already replaced) instead of pushing into
glib's NULL-queue warning.

--- a/common/ipc.c
+++ b/common/ipc.c
@@ -85,10 +85,11 @@
         if((ipc->channel != NULL) && (ipc->status == IPC_ENDPOINT_CONNECTED))
             g_io_channel_write_chars(ipc->channel, (gchar*)data, header->length, NULL, NULL);
 
-        if((ipc->channel != NULL) && (ipc->status == IPC_ENDPOINT_CONNECTED))
-            ipc_endpoint_decref(ipc);
-        else
+        if(!((ipc->channel != NULL) && (ipc->status == IPC_ENDPOINT_CONNECTED)))
             error("Trying to send an ipc message, but the endpoint went away.");
+        /* This message's reference is dropped on BOTH outcomes: the old
+         * went-away path leaked it, pinning dead endpoints forever. */
+        ipc_endpoint_decref(ipc);
 
         g_free(out);
     }
@@ -123,8 +124,14 @@
 
     if (ipc->channel)
         g_async_queue_push(send_queue, msg);
-    else
+    else if (ipc->queue)
         g_queue_push_tail(ipc->queue, msg);
+    else {
+        /* Endpoint already replaced/drained: drop the message rather than
+         * push onto a queue that no longer exists. */
+        g_free(msg);
+        ipc_endpoint_decref(ipc);
+    }
 }
 
 static void
@@ -213,8 +220,14 @@
 {
     g_assert(ipc->status == IPC_ENDPOINT_CONNECTED);
     g_assert(ipc->channel);
+    /* Tear the connection down NOW (this also removes both watches, so this
+     * handler cannot fire again), then drop the reference the connection
+     * took in ipc_endpoint_connect_to_socket().  The endpoint itself stays
+     * alive for as long as its owner (the webview) holds it; sends after
+     * this point queue harmlessly on ipc->queue. */
+    ipc_endpoint_disconnect(ipc);
     ipc_endpoint_decref(ipc);
-    return TRUE;
+    return FALSE;
 }
 
 void
@@ -299,6 +312,15 @@
      * g_io_channel_unix_new(), but before it has been set up fully */
     g_atomic_pointer_set(&ipc->channel, channel);
 
+    /* The CONNECTION holds its own reference, dropped by ipc_hup() when the
+     * peer goes away.  Without it, the hup handler's decref stole the
+     * owner's reference: one dead web process freed the endpoint while the
+     * webview still pointed at it, and every later send wrote through the
+     * dangling pointer into whatever had reused the slice -- observed as a
+     * deterministic crash in g_queue_push_tail and as heap corruption in
+     * the UI process whenever a web process crashed or was swapped. */
+    ipc_endpoint_incref_no_check(ipc);
+
     ipc->status = IPC_ENDPOINT_CONNECTED;
 
     if (!endpoints)
